# Issues and the board: gh dojo

This project tracks the issues in the current repository on the GitHub Projects board of acme, project 3, with the process in `.github/dojo.toml`. Take, move, comment on, block and create issues **only** through `gh dojo`: never change a status, a comment, a blocked-by link or an issue with `gh issue`, `gh project` or the API. For what gh dojo cannot do, such as editing an issue's title, body, labels or assignees, use `gh issue` only when the task needs it or the user asks.

Everything below is enough for everyday work; read `gh dojo <command> --help` only when a command refuses or you need a flag not named here. When the setup is broken (token, board, config), `gh dojo doctor` names each problem with the command that fixes it.

A bare `12` or `#12` is an issue of the current repository; an issue of another repository is `owner/repo#12` or its URL.

## Lifecycle

- A blocker is resolved, no longer blocking, only when it is closed; one whose status could not be read (`status not read: …`) still blocks.

## Verbs

Run a verb as `gh dojo <verb> N`; `--dry-run` prints its plan without changing anything, and `--show` also prints the issue's card, as `gh dojo show N` does. A verb adds the issue to the board if it is missing and refuses a closed issue unless noted. It confirms in one line: there is no need to read the issue again.

`N…` takes several numbers at once: one that fails is skipped and named in the summary.

**`go`** (Go): `gh dojo go N…`, any status → Doing.

## Reading

- `gh dojo next`: what to take next, the open issues without a status, with no unresolved blocker, in board order, 5 by default (`--limit N`). The blocked ones are hidden and counted; `--all` shows them with their blockers. Filters: `--mine`, `--label <label>`.
- `gh dojo show N` before you work on an issue: its status, blockers both ways, parent and sub-issues, history, description and the last 5 comments; `--comments all` prints the whole thread, `--full` everything uncut (a verb takes both too). Several numbers print three lines per issue.
- `gh dojo list`: an overview of the board, when the user asks for one: the open issues grouped by status; closed issues and final statuses are hidden and counted. Filters: `--status "<status>"`, `--status none`, `--mine`, `--label <label>`, `--all`, `--limit 100`.
- `gh dojo next` and `gh dojo list` read the board a few seconds behind writes: an issue just created or moved may be missing or in its old status. To check one issue, `gh dojo show N`.

## Creating and linking issues

- `gh dojo new "<title>" --type <label> --body "…"`: the issue, its labels, the board, in one command; a long body: `--body-file <file>`, or `--body-file -` for stdin. Also `--label <label>`, `--assignee @me`, `--milestone <title>`, `--blocked-by N`, `--parent N`, each checked before anything is created.
- `gh dojo comment N "text"`: a comment without changing the status.
- `gh dojo block N --by M` / `gh dojo unblock N --by M`: add or remove a blocked-by link without changing the status. An existing issue becomes a sub-issue with `gh issue edit N --parent M`.
- A commit pushed without a pull request names its issue after a keyword in its message, as `Fixes #12`; parentheses at the end of the first line, `(#12)`, do not count here: that is how `gh dojo release` finds the issues a release includes.

## Errors and repeats

On an error, do what its `fix:` line says. Exit code 2 is a process check refusing: never work around it (do not move the status another way); ask the user. On 4 (network, rate limit or auth) and 5 (partially applied), run the same command again, on 4 a bit later. A repeat is safe: what is already in place (a status, the latest comment, a link) is skipped, and `new` with the same title finishes the issue it created.
